{
    "attributes": {
        "action": "Allow self-employed single parents to claim childcare as business expense",
        "additional_details": "We believe HMRC rules penalise self-employed single parents ineligible for Universal Credit. Disallowing childcare as a business deduction can result in a double penalty during school holidays: parents may have to stop trading or pay holiday club fees from taxed net profits. Corporations can claim workplace childcare as a tax-deductible staff cost. Without childcare, self-employed parents may be unable to operate.  We feel childcare is an operational necessity, not a luxury. It should be an allowable business expense to ensure tax fairness.",
        "background": "Amend HMRC rules to allow self-employed single parents and self-employed households to deduct registered childcare and holiday club costs as an allowable business expense on their Self Assessment tax returns.",
